LoongArch: do not alias the scratch return buffer with the arguments

When ffi_call is given a NULL rvalue for a struct returned by reference,
ffi_call_int allocates a scratch buffer for it. The frame is laid out as
stack arguments (extra_bytes), the return buffer (rval_bytes), copies of
by-reference arguments (arg_bytes) and the call context, but the buffer
was placed at alloc_base + arg_bytes, as on RISC-V where arg_bytes is
the stack argument area. It then overlapped the first by-reference
struct copy, or the stack arguments, and the callee could read clobbered
arguments after writing its result.

Place it at alloc_base + extra_bytes, the space reserved for it.

--- a/src/loongarch/ffi.c
+++ b/src/loongarch/ffi.c
@@ -501,7 +501,7 @@
     alloc_base = FFI_ALIGN (alloca (alloc_size + STKALIGN - 1), STKALIGN);
 
   if (rval_bytes)
-    rvalue = (void *) (alloc_base + arg_bytes);
+    rvalue = (void *) (alloc_base + extra_bytes);
 
   call_builder cb;
   cb.used_float = cb.used_integer = 0;
